 name its real colours, type and spacing so the design matches rather than inventing a new look. End the brief by saying the canvas payload     [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mmust be saved as `plays/006-sheet-improvements/design/canvas.html`.                                                                                                               [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mKeep it compact — it is passed to the design skill as a single line, so write it as flowing prose with no headings, no bullet characters and no code blocks. Then end your turn   [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mwith one short statement saying the brief is ready. NOT a question: that statement is what starts the design run.                                                                 [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mSTEP 5 — Once the design skill has produced the canvas, leave the design in two places, both of which matter:                                                                     [39m  [38;2;153;153;153m1. `plays/006-sheet-improvements/design/design.md` — the published canvas URL, then one line per screen saying what it is and what it does. The Planner and the Developer read    [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mthis file; keep it short and factual.                                                                                                                                             [39m  [38;2;153;153;153m2. `documentation/design/006-sheet-improvements-prototype.html` — how I actually LOOK at the prototype, from the Documentation tab of the project screen. Create the folder if it [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mis not there. This file is rendered inside a sandboxed iframe with NO scripting, NO navigation and NO network, so it must be ONE self-contained page: every artboard's markup     [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mstacked one under another with a heading naming the screen, every style inline or in a `<style>` block in that same file, no script tags, and no references to any external file, [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mfont or image (inline any image as a data URI). Put the canvas URL at the top as plain visible text as well as a link — a link alone cannot be opened from that sandbox. Do NOT   [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mcopy the canvas payload itself here; it needs JavaScript and would render as a blank page.                                                                                        [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mIf the canvas cannot be PUBLISHED, that is fine: keep the artboards the skill produced, still write both documents from them, and say plainly that the canvas was not published.  [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mThat is the only fallback. If the design skill did not run at all, do NOT hand-write a canvas file or a prototype in its place — say plainly that the skill did not run and stop, [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mso the problem is visible instead of being papered over with something that only looks like a design.                                                                             [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mSTEP 6 — Hand the prototype to me for review, and STOP. Once both documents are written, end your turn by telling me in a few lines what you designed and where I can look at it, [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mand then ASKING me to review the prototype and confirm it is what I want before you plan the implementation. End that turn on that question and wait — planning does not start    [39m  [38;2;153;153;153muntil I have approved the design.                                                                                                                                                 [39m  [38;2;153;153;153m- If I ask for changes, revise the design and both documents, then ask me again. Keep ending those turns on a question.                                                           [39m  [38;2;153;153;153m- Only once I have actually approved it, end your turn with a single short statement saying the design is approved. That statement — and nothing else — is what starts the        [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mplanning session. Never write it while I still owe you an answer.                                                                                                                 [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mYou are designing only. Do not modify anything in the repositories, do not implement anything, and do not write `plays/006-sheet-improvements/plan.md`.                           [39m                                                                                                                                                                                      [38;2;153;153;153mPROJECT CONTEXT FILES: this project keeps its documentation under `documentation/`; the documents in `documentation/context/` are shared by every play and apply to all work.     [39m  [38;2;153;153;153mRead ALL of them before doing anything and make your work conform to them: `documentation/context/vision.md` (the problem, users, and product direction),                         [39m  [38;2;153;153;153m`documentation/context/tech.md` (overall architecture plus